  "worked_solution": "For a surface $S$ defined by $x=f(y,z)$ with $(y,z)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222c}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn this case, our surface is given by\n\n$$\nx={y}^{3}+{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})=3{y}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202z} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}({y}^{3}+{e}^{z})={e}^{z},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\u2202x}{\u2202y})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\u2202y}{\u2202z})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(3{y}^{2})}^{2}+({e}^{z})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{D}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$\\,$ $\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}+{e}^{2z}}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}\\le 1.$",
